course: dictionary
lesson: 9567
title: Straitened times
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Straiten is a rare, formal word. You will mostly meet it in one phrase: «in straitened circumstances». Circumstances means the situation a person is in, such as how much money they have.
explain: Read the clues around the phrase. It turns up in old books and stiff, formal writing, and it tells you that a family or person is short of money. If a story says a family lived in straitened circumstances, you can guess they were poor, even before you check a dictionary.
explain: In the phrase, straitened describes the circumstances, so it works like an adjective. Straiten can also be a verb, meaning to make someone poorer or to make something narrow or tight. Its old meaning is less common today.
explain: In everyday talk, people say hard up, short of money or broke. Use those words when you speak, and save straitened for reading old books and formal writing.
fact: Straiten is a rare, formal word.
fact: «In straitened circumstances» means poor and short of money.
fact: Straiten can mean to make someone poorer or to limit what they have.
fact: Old use: straiten can mean to make something narrow or tight.
fact: Everyday words for the same idea are hard up, short of money and broke.
exercise: The phrase «in straitened circumstances» means what? | Lost in a forest | *Short of money | Very tall and thin | Full of food and happy
exercise: Which everyday words mean nearly the same as the formal phrase «in straitened circumstances»? | Rich and famous | Wide awake | *Hard up | Very hungry
exercise: In an old book, a road is said to be straitened by fallen trees. What does that most likely mean? | Lit up with lamps | Painted bright red | Made wider and smoother | *Made narrow, so there is less room to pass
test: «The family was in straitened circumstances after the shop closed.» What does this tell you? | *They had very little money | They had too much money to spend | They were moving house to the seaside | They were very happy
test: Which word is the closest opposite of «straitened circumstances»? | Narrow | Glum | *Wealthy | Hungry
exam: Jayden finds «in straitened circumstances» in an old book. What is the best way to work out its meaning? | Look only at the first letter | *Use the context clues and swap in hard up or short of money | Guess it means happy because it sounds nice | Skip it and never read the book again
